@page "/docs/accessibility/pointer-and-touch"

@* WCAG 2.5.8 target size (the sweep of Nov 10, 2026) and 2.5.7 dragging (inventory section 1.10), with
   what changes on touch screens (section 1.13). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="pointer-and-touch">
    <Lead>
        Click targets are at least 24 by 24 pixels, or have room around them, as WCAG 2.2 requires. The one exception is
        listed below. Every drag also has a single-click alternative, through a button or a menu.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Target sizes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Buttons and icon buttons are at least 32 pixels tall at the small size, and 28 at Compact density. A click anywhere on the label of a
            checkbox, radio or switch toggles it. Small icons inside a field or a tab, such as a clear button or a tab's close
            button, look small but accept clicks across a 24-pixel area around them.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            One control is smaller than 24 pixels: the stacked step buttons of a
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/number-field">NumberField</Anchor>. The user can type the number instead. To show
            full-height buttons on each side, set <Code>StepButtons="StepButtons.Split"</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A way around every drag</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Dragging is hard or impossible for some users, so every drag has a single-click alternative with the same
            result:
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Drags" Caption="Drags and their single-click alternatives" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-wrap" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Drag">@context!.Drag</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Without dragging">@context!.Instead</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">On touch screens</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Fields use 16-pixel text on touch screens, so iOS doesn't zoom the page when a field gets focus.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Date fields open the phone's native date picker. A date field that needs IzzyBlazor's calendar shows its calendar button instead.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>File uploads hide the "or drag it here" text.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Over a chart, one finger scrolls the page and two fingers pinch to zoom the chart.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The user can swipe a carousel, and its arrows and dots still work.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Toasts don't pause on touch screens, because there's no hover. Error toasts stay until the user dismisses them.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
